WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:15.766
Stem Cell Politics: The Michigan Initiative

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:20.499
My name is Sean Morrison and I direct the University of Michigan Center for Stem Cell Biology

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.799
The state of Michigan had been one of the states in this country

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.099
with the most restrictive laws when it comes to embryonic stem cell research.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:34.532
It was legal in Michigan to throw embryos away that had been created

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.566
during in vitro fertilization

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:41.066
and in fact, for a variety of reasons, large numbers of embryos

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:42.966
are routinely discarded from those clinics

00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:45.699
that cannot be used for fertility treatment.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.632
So, it was legal to throw embryos away,

00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:51.666
but it was not legal to take the embryos that were being thrown away

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:54.666
and use them to drive embryonic stem cell lines

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:58.699
that could be used in medical research that might one day help patients.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.099
This was really blocking the ability of stem cell research to develop

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.332
in the state of Michigan in a way that other states

00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:08.832
with major research universities didn't have to content with.

00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:10.966
So we, within the University of Michigan,

00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:14.266
worked to try to improve those state laws.

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:16.866
Initially, by trying to work through the legislature,

00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:20.099
and when that didn't work, we went straight to the people of Michigan,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.799
knowing that there was broad bipartisan support among the people of the state

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.999
for expanded embryonic stem cell research,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.132
much like in other states.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:34.066
It turns out that we as scientists have to be involved

00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:36.099
in the public policy process

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.966
because if you're not involved, you get bad laws for bad reasons.

00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:43.766
Policies end up being created partly out of ignorance,

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.899
partly out of other agendas that can oftentimes

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.699
destroy opportunites to make people's lives better.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.732
So, during the proposal to campaign, we spent a lot of time

00:01:56.733 --> 00:02:01.432
thinking about how to explain what stems cell research really was,

00:02:01.433 --> 00:02:05.099
what was at stake, how it could be done more effectively,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.999
and how it would potentially help the people of Michigan.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.366
Part of that process was in polling and doing focus groups

00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:16.166
which tried to understand what the public understood,

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:18.399
what the public thought about the research,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.699
what aspects of the research needed to be explained more,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.699
and how we could best help people to understand.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.566
And in that process, there were a lot of surprises about what the public really understood,

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:33.866
what the public was worried about, and how we could best understand it.

00:02:33.867 --> 00:02:39.366
Unfortunately, our opponents were also, of course, doing polling and focus groups,

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:44.132
except they were looking for what they could use to most frighten the public‚Äî

00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:47.632
to cause people to not support the ballot proposal.

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:53.466
Politics 101 is if you want people to vote against an issue, you frighten and confuse them.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:57.366
And so, sadly, the opponents of the proposal, to campaign,

00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:01.566
when they realized in their own focus groups and polling that

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:05.999
none of the true messages about what the proposal was really about

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.666
actually resonated with public and actually frightened them,

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:12.599
they decided to run a 100% false campaign

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.466
where they made stuff up to frighten and confuse the public that just wasn't true.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:21.866
Their first message was, "Vote against Proposal 2 because it'll increase your taxes."

00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:24.699
Well, there was no funding associated with the proposal.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.966
There was nothing - it had nothing to do with taxes.

00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:31.366
But, they learned in their polling that people were frightened of new taxes.

00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:37.932
Their second message was that crazy scientists wanted to mix human DNA with animal eggs

00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:41.299
to try to clone half-people, half-animals.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.632
and they would dress actors up in cow suits and put them on TV

00:03:45.633 --> 00:03:48.299
to try to frighten people about this brave new world

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.366
that they would create by voting for Proposal 2.

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:56.099
In fact, again, it was obviously a false and ludicrous suggestion,

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.599
particularly given that cloning remains illegal in the state of Michigan.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:05.932
Prop 2 dealt with the ability to give patients the right to donate for research

00:04:05.933 --> 00:04:08.966
embryos that were created by in vitro fertilization

00:04:08.967 --> 00:04:12.932
and that would otherwise be discarded if not donated for research.

00:04:12.933 --> 00:04:16.066
So it put that right back into patients hands,

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:18.699
as it is in most other states within the country,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.532
to decide what they do with their own embryos,

00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:24.766
rather than leaving those decisions with the state legislature.

00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:31.799
Finally, they argued that stem cell research was akin to the Tuskegee syphilis experiments,

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.332
which was a terrible period in the history of American medical research

00:04:36.333 --> 00:04:43.166
in which minority sharecroppers were victimized in the course of medical research experiments.

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:45.999
But, of course, that was a turning point in the development

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.566
of our current notions about medical ethics and patient protection,

00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:52.132
and nothing like that could ever happen again,

00:04:52.133 --> 00:04:55.532
as a result of Federal laws, and other kinds of state laws,

00:04:55.533 --> 00:05:00.599
and the more sophisticated notions we have about what medical research should be.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.099
And so there was no truth to that claim,

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.566
but yet they felt that had an opportunity to frighten the voters.

00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:10.332
Well, there was widespread coverage in the media,

00:05:10.333 --> 00:05:15.766
partly because of the efforts of scientists to explain what the research was really about

00:05:15.767 --> 00:05:17.732
and why the opposition claims were false.

00:05:17.733 --> 00:05:23.099
There was widespread coverage in the media about how false the opposition claim was

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.799
and it eventually, during the campaign, engendered anger in the general population

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:34.199
about how this special interest group was trying to defeat this proposal

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:39.699
by tricking the voters into voting against their own self interest

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:42.199
and against their own values.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:46.332
What we learned as a result of that campaign

00:05:46.333 --> 00:05:51.266
is that hope and truth prevail over fear and misinformation

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:54.299
and that scientists can play a really critical role

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.032
in making sure that the right thing happens in the end

00:05:57.033 --> 00:06:03.866
by being clear and by spending the time to explain the research to the general public.

00:06:03.867 --> 00:06:08.999
One of the interesting things that we learned

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.499
as a result of the polling that we did prior to the campaign

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:17.132
was that we asked people to rank sources of information in terms of credibility.

00:06:17.133 --> 00:06:20.232
And, we listed ten different sources of information:

00:06:20.233 --> 00:06:26.899
Your friends and family, your physician, your pastor, university professors, etc.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.832
And, number one in terms of credibility was university professors

00:06:30.833 --> 00:06:36.032
University professors are perceived as people who are in the business of education,

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:42.566
and who are trying to explain things to the public, and so it's important for us to get engaged.

00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:46.732
And as a consequence, we had the presumption of credibility

00:06:46.733 --> 00:06:51.066
when we engaged with the merchants of fear and misinformation in the debate.

00:06:51.067 --> 00:06:59.699
And so, we owned the moral high ground in this debate

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:04.766
because we had the capacity to defend our position by telling the truth

00:07:04.767 --> 00:07:07.432
and the more the truth came out during the debate,

00:07:07.433 --> 00:07:09.266
the more likely we were to win.

00:07:09.267 --> 00:07:12.332
Whereas the opposition had to lie in order to

00:07:12.333 --> 00:07:14.599
convince people to vote in the way that they wanted.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:20.199
Well, according to every rule of thumb in Michigan politics, we should have lost that campaign.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.866
The rule of thumb is that if you want people to vote yes on a proposal,

00:07:22.867 --> 00:07:25.166
you have to outspend the opponents 2:1.

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:29.232
Well, it was 1:1 because we were up against a well-funded opposition.

00:07:29.233 --> 00:07:32.432
The rule of thumb is that you have to go into the election day

00:07:32.433 --> 00:07:37.932
with about a 10% lead in the polls because undecided voters

00:07:37.933 --> 00:07:41.066
break disproporionately to No on election day.

00:07:41.067 --> 00:07:44.866
And yet, we were going in neck and neck into election day.

00:07:44.867 --> 00:07:49.432
So, the day before the election, I saw comments from 10 different political pundits,

00:07:49.433 --> 00:07:52.832
9 of whom said the proposal was going to go down to defeat.

00:07:52.833 --> 00:07:56.732
And yet, three different news organizations did exit polling

00:07:56.733 --> 00:07:58.732
throughout the state on the day of the election,

00:07:58.733 --> 00:08:04.099
and in every case, the exit polls were so clearly trending toward the Yes side

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:09.499
that all three news organizations unofficially called it in our favor

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:12.199
when the polls closed at 8pm that night.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.766
So it was a remarkable experience because we could see

00:08:15.767 --> 00:08:19.799
that when you could explain things clearly to the public,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:23.399
the public would have an opportunity to really

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:26.132
make up their mind based on the merits of the issue

00:08:26.133 --> 00:08:29.132
and to see through the tactics of fear and confusion

00:08:29.133 --> 00:08:31.266
that often pervade political debates.

00:08:31.267 --> 00:08:35.099
And going forward in this area, it will be particularly important

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:40.232
for more scientists to be engaged in these kinds of processes,

00:08:40.233 --> 00:08:43.666
both to defend the public investment in medical research,

00:08:43.667 --> 00:08:45.532
as well as to create effective laws.

00:08:45.533 --> 00:08:49.666
And as young people come up through the system and become scientists,

00:08:49.667 --> 00:08:53.032
for them to understand that part of their job is

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:55.999
not just to work in the laboratory and to do their work,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:06.366
but to explain to the public why it's valuable for the public to invest in that work.

00:09:06.367 --> 00:09:09.232
